-- title:
--   Gauss residue fields at the floor: X₁(M) and j(q),j(q^M)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $M$ a nonzero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of order $p$, and let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Inside $L((q))$ consider the intermediate fields $K_1$, equal to the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion field $\mathbb{Q}$-adjoined by the integral form ratios for $\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$, and $K_2$, generated likewise from the $q$-expansion field for $\Gamma_0(Mp)$, with $K_2 \le K_1$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as its fraction field, with $p$ in its maximal ideal, with $\zeta$ in the image of $A$, compatibly an $A$-algebra structure on $K_1$ and on $K_2$ over $L$, and let $\varpi$ generate the maximal ideal. Let $j \in K_1$ and $j_2 \in K_2$ be nonzero elements whose $q$-expansion is the image of $jq = q^{-1}\,jNumQ$, and $j_M \in K_2$ an element whose $q$-expansion is the image of $jq$ after $q \mapsto q^M$. Let $W_0$ be a valuation subring of $K_1$ consisting exactly of those $f$ admitting a Gauss presentation $f \cdot \hat y = \hat x$ with $x,y \in A[[q]]$, the reduction of $y$ nonzero, and hats denoting coefficientwise images in $L((q))$; let $W_2 \subseteq K_2$ be the preimage of $W_0$ under the inclusion $K_2 \hookrightarrow K_1$, with a compatible $W_2$-algebra structure on $W_0$; assume the images of $A$ lie in $W_0$ and $W_2$, and that $j \in W_0$, $j_2, j_M \in W_2$. Writing $\kappa$ for the residue field of $A$, the conclusion asserts the existence of ring isomorphisms $\varepsilon_1$ from the residue field of $W_0$ onto the $\kappa$-subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by the integral form ratios for $\Gamma_1(M)$, and $\varepsilon_2$ from the residue field of $W_2$ onto the $\kappa$-subfield of $\kappa((q))$ generated by $\bar j(q)$ and $\bar j(q^M)$, such that: $\varepsilon_1$ and $\varepsilon_2$ agree as maps into $\kappa((q))$ on residues of elements of $W_2$; $\varepsilon_1$ sends the residue of $j$ to $\bar j(q)$, while $\varepsilon_2$ sends the residues of $j_2$ and of $j_M$ to the distinguished generators $\bar j(q)$ and $\bar j(q^M)$; both maps are Gauss reduction, in that any presentation $e \cdot \hat y = \hat x$ as above reduces to $\varepsilon_i(\bar e) \cdot \bar y = \bar x$ in $\kappa((q))$; both restrict on residues of elements of $A$ to the residue map $A \to \kappa$ followed by the structure map; and the field generated by $\bar j(q), \bar j(q^M)$ is contained in the one generated by the $\Gamma_1(M)$ form ratios.
--
--   This is the identification of the two Gauss (i.e. $\infty$-sheet) residue fields at level $\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_0(p)$ and at level $\Gamma_0(Mp)$ with the characteristic-$p$ $q$-expansion function fields of $X_1(M)$ and of $j(q), j(q^M)$, compatibly with the inclusion of fields and with the modular invariants; it is the function-field counterpart of the Deligne–Rapoport description of the special fibre at $p$ of $\Gamma_0(p)$-structures. It feeds the computation of the inertia at places of the $\Gamma_0(Mp)$ model.
